"],["Colombard",370,"W","Colombard"],["Ugni Blanc",140,"W","Trebbiano Toscano"],["Chardonnay",71,"W","Chardonnay"],["Chenin Blanc",13,"W","Chenin Blanc"],["Other",13,"","other"]],"legal":["main","main","main","main","main","main","main","main","main","main","no","no",""]},"area_km2":7011,"large":false,"reading":{"climate":[["Grown here","Merlot, Cabernet Sauvignon, Cabernet Franc and Sémillon sit in their sweet spot, so the wines can show the style they are known for."]]},"winkler":["Ib",""],"terms":[["AOC / AOP","France's protected place names. A wine sold under one must follow that appellation's rules. The EU's term for the same tier is PDO, or AOP in French."],["IGP","Indication Géographique Protégée, the tier below AOC. It replaced vin de pays in 2009."],["Vin de France","France's simplest category, in use since 2010. It replaced vin de table."],["Old vines, Vieilles vignes","There is no legal or agreed definition of old, so the words promise nothing on their own."],["Bordeaux Supérieur","One of the two regional appellations allowed anywhere in Bordeaux, beside plain Bordeaux. Together they cover about half the Bordeaux vineyard."],["Cru Classé (1855)","In 1855 brokers ranked Bordeaux estates by reputation and price. Reds were ranked from first to fifth growths, nearly all in the Médoc, and the sweet whites of Sauternes and Barsac from superior first growth to second growth."]],"keyfacts":["The Atlantic, the Gironde estuary and the Garonne and Dordogne rivers moderate Bordeaux's oceanic climate, and gravel terraces along the rivers are well drained and warm.","Château Mouton Rothschild was raised to first growth in 1973, and Château d'Yquem is the only superior first growth of Sauternes.","Saint-Émilion was classified in 1955 and is updated about every ten years, Graves in 1953 and 1959, and Pomerol has no official classification."],"style":{"notes":["Most Bordeaux is red, with sweet and dry whites, rosé and sparkling Crémant de Bordeaux making up the rest.","Plain Bordeaux AOC wines are fruity, with little oak, and are made to drink young.","Left Bank reds lean on Cabernet Sauvignon and are concentrated, tannic and long lived.","Right Bank reds lean on Merlot and often have great fruit concentration and softer tannins.","Graves is best known for dry whites, and Sauternes for some of the world's most famous sweet wines."]},"blurb":{"text":"A southwest France wine region around the city of Bordeaux, where the Dordogne joins the Garonne to form the Gironde estuary. The Gironde grows mostly Merlot (58 percent), Cabernet Sauvignon (20) and Cabernet Franc (8), with some Sémillon and Sauvignon Blanc."},"study":true,"levels":[2,3],"summary":{"places":44,"kinds":[["PDO",38],["region",6]],"grapes":[["Cabernet Franc","red",29],["Merlot","red",29],["Cabernet Sauvignon","red",28],["Malbec","red",28],["Sauvignon Blanc","white",19],["Sauvignon Gris","pink",19],["Sémillon","white",19],["Muscadelle","white",19],["Carmenère","red",17],["Petit Verdot","red",17]],"with_rules":38,"gst":[16.3,16.9],"best":["france/sauternes","france/sauternes-barsac","france/left-bank","france/saint-emilion","france/pomerol","france/graves","france/right-bank","france/saint-estephe"]},"sources":[{"name":"Wikidata sitelinks and Wikimedia page views","url":"https://www.wikidata.org"},{"name":"Candiago et al. 2022, A geospatial inventory of regulatory information for wine protected designations of origin in Europe, Scientific Data (product specifications as of 2021)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-022-01513-0"},{"name":"INAO, the appellation's cahier des charges, chapter V (grapes and their shares), from the Bulletin officiel du ministère de l'Agriculture (Licence Ouverte)","url":"https://www.inao.gouv.fr/rechercher-un-produit"},{"name":"Fick and Hijmans 2017, WorldClim 2.1 monthly averages for 1970 to 2000, 2.5 minute grid (about 4.5 km)","url":"https://www.worldclim.org/data/worldclim21.html"},{"name":"Beck et al. 2023, High-resolution (1 km) Koppen-Geig